A||N||8||Seanh||Why does my auto tranny do this?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||04-27-2001||09:09 PM||||I was just wondering if this is normal or what, but when I floor my car off the line the damn tranny doesn't shift outta first until like 4700 rpm and then won't shift into third until I hit about 4600rpm's in second. Is that normal? It seems like I would have better performance if it shifted sooner. Would one of the auto tranny shift kits from the fiero store help it any? Perhaps just changing the fluid will be good? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Spektyr||04-27-2001||09:17 PM||||Not real familiar with the Fiero auto trannys, but on my 4 speed GT I don't shift until about 5k if I want to really go fast.  The V6 redlines at 6k, with a yellow band between 5.5 and 6, and she's still pulling plenty hard at those RPM.<P>One thing you can do if you want it to shift sooner is let off the throttle slightly until it shifts (practice will let you know exactly how much), then slowly reapply the throttle to max.<P>But peak HP comes pretty high on these motors.  It won't hurt them to run them fast, just don't exceed the redline.  (assuming there's no other problems with the motor)<P>In my 4 speed I can't really shift below 4k rpm if my foot is on the floor.  By the time I've let the clutch out, I'm already at 4 on the tach, and half a second later I have to shift to avoid redline.  My total time in 1st gear under full throttle is maybe 1-1.5 seconds.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||04-27-2001||09:55 PM||||higher revs is lots safer on engine than lugging it in too high a gear. One reason old VW bugs blew so many engines, people were afraid to rev em.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-27-2001||11:01 PM||||normal<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-27-2001||11:09 PM||||If you are standing on a 125 it will hold gears quite deep. First thing to check is the TV cable that runs between the throttle and tranny. If that's not ajusted right shifting will be off.<P>Changing the fluid is always good. Do that before considering a kit. One method of extracting the old oil is in my cave.<A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shaun41178||04-28-2001||12:29 AM||||your tranny is shifting way too early<P>Mine shifts at 5500 rpm at WOT every time.  Even with the nitrous.<P>You need to adjust something so it shifts around 5200-5500 rpm at WOT.  If it shifts sooner then 4700 rpm like you want then that is way to early and you will be out of your powerband.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||04-28-2001||01:13 AM||||Wow, and I thought it was shifting to late. I'm just not used to this car yet. My 86 GT has the 4spd auto in it, and I have never driven a stock fiero auto tranny car until I bought the 87GT a few weeks back. However, I see an LS1 in this new cars future.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||04-28-2001||10:09 AM||||I'm with Shaun... My '87 GT shifts at 5500rpm at WOT.  I love it!  If it didn't I'd be screwed since my Crane cam is ground to make power at higher RPM's.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||04-28-2001||10:20 AM||||Actually, you are probally right about the rpm's. I usally get weary and back off the gas a little to let it shift.||||reg||1
